.TH man 7 "13 Oct 2024" "v4" "LDMS Plugin dynamic_stream_sampler man page"

.SH NAME
Plugin_dynamic_stream_sampler - man page for the LDMS dynamic_stream_sampler plugin

.SH SYNOPSIS
Within ldmsd_controller or a configuration file:
.br
config name=dynamic_stream_sampler [ <attr>=<value> ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
With LDMS (Lightweight Distributed Metric Service), plugins for the ldmsd (ldms daemon) are configured via ldmsd_controller
or a configuration file. The dynamic_stream_sampler plugin does not actually sample, but rather subscribes to an ldmsd_stream that is meant to take commands. When it gets a request on the command channel to subscribe to a new stream, it will call subscribe on its ldmsd from the upstream L1 <HOW DOES IT FIND THIS?>. It will also tell its aggregator (L1) to subscribe to that stream from L2 <How does anyone find the next level? does the L1 also have a sampler?>. The request to subscribe will come from the app who is publishing to the stream already.

The sampler is on the ldmsd on the node. HOW DOES IT FIND ITS L1? WHO NEEDS TO FIND THE L2? Is there a sampler on the L1s and L2s subscribing to cmd? Are these the same samplers (how)?

.SH CONFIGURATION ATTRIBUTE SYNTAX
The dynamic_stream_sampler plugin uses the sampler_base base class. This man page covers only the configuration attributes, or those with default values, specific to the this plugin; see ldms_sampler_base.man for the attributes of the base class.

.TP
.BR config
name=<plugin_name> stream=<stream>
.br
configuration line
.RS
.TP
name=<plugin_name>
.br
This MUST be dynamic_stream_sampler.
.TP
stream=<stream>
.br
Name of the stream to which to subscribe.
.RE

.SH BUGS
This is under development and is junk.

.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
THESE ARE NOT CURRENT
Within ldmsd_controller or a configuration file:
.nf
load name=hello_sampler
config name=hello_sampler producer=host1 instance=host1/hello_sampler stream=foo component_id=1
start name=hello_sampler interval=1000000 offset=0
.fi

.PP
.nf
> ./hello_publisher -x sock -h localhost -p 16000 -a munge -s foo -m "foo" -t str
The data was successfully published.
The server responded with 0

> ./hello_publisher -x sock -h localhost -p 16000 -a munge -s foo -m "bar" -t str
The data was successfully published.
The server responded with 0
.ni

.PP
In the log file of the ldmsd:
.nf
> cat log.txt
Mon May 04 19:44:05 2020: CRITICAL  : stream_type: STRING, msg: "foo", msg_len: 4, entity: (nil)
Mon May 04 19:44:24 2020: CRITICAL  : stream_type: STRING, msg: "bar", msg_len: 4, entity: (nil)
.ni

Note that the hello_streams sampler does not do a sample, instead it subscribes to the stream with a callback a\
nd prints out what it got off the stream.
.fi


